before:
```typescript
type Named = { name: string };
type Counted = { count: number };
type Stock = Named & Counted;
function stockText(stock: Stock): string {
  return stock.name + ": " + stock.count;
}
console.log(stockText({ name: "Pencils", count: 4 }));
```
This prints Pencils: 4. stockText accepts one Stock object and returns text. The colon after stock names its parameter type; ): string names the return type. Dot access reads name and count. + joins those values into the returned text. console.log receives that result and displays it.

```typescript
type Named = { name: string };
type Counted = { count: number };
type Stock = Named & Counted;
function stockText(stock: Stock): string {
  return stock.name + ": " + stock.count;
}
console.log(stockText({ name: "Clips", count: 0 }));
```
This prints Clips: 0. Trace the changed value through the same rule.

after:
Stock needs both a name and a count.
```typescript
type Named = { name: string };
type Counted = { count: number };
type Stock = Named & Counted;
function stockText(stock: Stock): string {
  return stock.name + ": " + stock.count;
}
console.log(stockText({ name: "Pencils", count: 4 }));
```
The output is Pencils: 4.
